# Line-ending policy.
#
# The index already stores pure LF (verified via `git ls-files --eol`), so this
# file codifies existing behavior rather than changing it. Without it, correct
# checkouts depend on each contributor setting core.autocrlf themselves.

# Default: git decides what is text, stores LF in the index, and converts to the
# platform's native ending on checkout.
* text=auto

# Must be LF even on Windows checkouts. CRLF here produces
# "bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M" the moment these run on Linux -- in a container,
# in CI, or under WSL. Dockerfile:17 (`COPY . .`) copies the worktree verbatim
# into the image, so a CRLF working copy would carry straight through.
*.sh            text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
Procfile        text eol=lf
*.env.example   text eol=lf
.nvmrc          text eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts need the opposite: CRLF is required by cmd.exe.
*.bat           text eol=crlf
*.cmd           text eol=crlf
*.ps1           text eol=crlf

# Never apply line-ending conversion to binaries. `text=auto` detects most of
# these on its own; declaring them makes it explicit and immune to heuristics.
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.gif           binary
*.ico           binary
*.pdf           binary
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary
*.ttf           binary
*.eot           binary
*.wasm          binary
*.zip           binary
*.gz            binary

# Generated: collapse in PR diffs and skip language statistics.
package-lock.json   linguist-generated=true -diff
*.map               linguist-generated=true -diff
